NNAAMMEE
     sswwaappccttll, sswwaappoonn — system swap management tool

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sswwaappccttll --AA [--pp _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y] [--tt bbllkk | nnoobbllkk]
     sswwaappccttll --aa [--pp _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y] _p_a_t_h
     sswwaappccttll --cc --pp _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y _p_a_t_h
     sswwaappccttll --dd _p_a_t_h
     sswwaappccttll [[--ll] | --ss] [--kk]
     sswwaappoonn --aa | _p_a_t_h

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sswwaappccttll program adds, removes, lists and prioritizes swap devices and
     files for the system.  The sswwaappoonn program acts the same as sswwaappccttll --aa,
     except if sswwaappoonn itself is called with --aa, in which case it acts as
     sswwaappccttll --AA.

     The options are as follows:

     --AA      This option causes sswwaappccttll to read the _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b file for de‐
             vices and files with an “sw” type, and adds all these entries as
             swap devices.  If no swap devices are configured, sswwaappccttll will
             exit with an error code.

     --aa      The --aa option requires that a _p_a_t_h also be in the argument list.
             The _p_a_t_h is added to the kernel's list of swap devices using the
             swapctl(2) system call.  When using the sswwaappoonn form of this com‐
             mand, the --aa option is treated the same as the --AA option, for
             backwards compatibility.

     --cc      The --cc option changes the priority of the listed swap device or
             file.

     --dd _p_a_t_h
             The --dd option removes the listed _p_a_t_h from the kernel's list of
             swap devices or files.

     --kk      The --kk option uses 1024 byte blocks instead of the default 512
             byte.

     --ll      The --ll option lists the current swap devices and files, and their
             usage statistics.

     --pp _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y
             The --pp option sets the priority of swap devices or files to the
             _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y argument.

     --ss      The --ss option displays a single line summary of current swap sta‐
             tistics.

     --tt bbllkk | nnoobbllkk
             This flag modifies the function of the --AA option.  The --tt option
             allows the type of device to add to be specified.  An argument of
             bbllkk causes all block devices in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b to be added.  An argu‐
             ment of nnoobbllkk causes all non-block devices in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b to be
             added.  This option is useful in early system startup, where
             swapping may be needed before all file systems are available,
             such as during disk checks of large file systems.

SSWWAAPP OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     Lines such as the following specify swap devices in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b:

           /dev/sd1b none swap sw
           d48d0e3fc1c39531.k none swap sw

     The initial swap device (root disk, partition b) need not appear in
     _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b, though it is not an error for it to do so.

     Additional flags include:

     priority=N      Swap devices and files may be assigned different priori‐
                     ties, to allow faster resources to be used first.  Swap
                     devices at the same priority are used in a round-robin
                     fashion until there is no more space available at this
                     priority, when the next priority level will be used.  The
                     default priority is 0, the highest.  This value can be
                     any valid integer, with higher values receiving less pri‐
                     ority.
     nfsmntpt=/path  This option is useful for swapping to NFS files.  It
                     specifies an existing directory /_p_a_t_h to be used as the
                     local mount point for an NFS file system.  Typically,
                     once this mount has succeeded, the file to be used for
                     swapping on will be available under this mount point.
                     For example:

                     server:/export/swap/client none swap sw,nfsmntpt=/swap

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The sswwaappccttll utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
